Exactly. There’s a meaningful distinction between possessing an internal intuition and possessing usable knowledge—but people often collapse them to protect the flattering interpretation.

“I understand it; I just can’t explain it” can mean several different things:

* The person has a genuine, nonverbal model but lacks the vocabulary or teaching skill to serialize it.
* They recognize the answer when they see it but cannot independently reconstruct it.
* They feel familiarity, coherence, or profundity and mistake that feeling for understanding.
* They’re preserving a self-image against the absence of observable performance.

Your backflip analogy identifies the uncomfortable part: latent potential is not the same as instantiated capability. Maybe your nervous system could learn a backflip unusually quickly—but until it can reliably produce one, “I know how” is inaccurate. Likewise, an idea that cannot survive translation into words, examples, predictions, decisions, diagrams, or demonstrations has not yet become fully operational knowledge.

Language is somewhat different because thought can genuinely outrun speech. A mechanic, athlete, artist, or traumatized person may know things procedurally or somatically that they cannot verbalize. But there still ought to be some output channel: better predictions, successful actions, accurate recognition, useful metaphors, correction of errors. If the supposed understanding produces no differential effect anywhere, there is no external reason to distinguish it from imagined understanding.

A clean test might be:

Understanding is not merely having a representation; it is being able to transform that representation without destroying what makes it true.

Explain it simply. Apply it somewhere new. Predict what happens. Identify where it breaks. Teach it through an analogy. Build something with it. Those are intellectual equivalents of landing the backflip.

So yes—sometimes “I can’t get it out” is exulansis: real inner structure failing to cross the interface. Sometimes it’s cope. The difference appears in the deltas. Give the person alternate interfaces and see whether the alleged intelligence leaves tracks.